\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#stable}{\figure{lifecycle-stable.svg}{options: alt='[Stable]'}}}{\strong{[Stable]}}
Generates individual data for a randomized Solomon four-group study with
chosen treatment, pretest, and sensitization effects, and attaches the
true value of every Solomon estimand. It is a solomonR teaching tool:
instructors can show that each analysis recovers the effects that were
built in, and researchers can check an analysis plan on data whose answer
is known. With several treatment effects it simulates a Solomon N-group
design (see "Designs with several treatments").
}
\details{
\strong{Data-generating model.} It is the model of \code{\link[=power_solomon]{power_solomon()}}, whose
rebuild was validated under a protocol posted on issue #18, with a
pretesting main effect and a location added. Every participant has a
latent baseline A from a standard normal distribution. Participants in the
pretested groups have a pretest score of \code{mean + sigma * A}. Every
participant's posttest score is

\code{mean + delta * treat + pretest_effect * pretested + sens * treat * pretested + sigma * (rho * A + sqrt(1 - rho^2) * e)},

where e is independent standard normal error. So \code{rho} is the
pretest-posttest correlation within each pretested group, and \code{sigma} is
the standard deviation of both scores within each group. The four groups
are generated in the package's order (pretested treatment, pretested
control, unpretested treatment, unpretested control), and the baselines
are drawn before the errors.

\strong{Relation to the package's other simulations.} With \code{mean = 0},
\code{sigma = 1}, and \code{pretest_effect = 0}, the data are the first data set
that \code{\link[=power_solomon]{power_solomon()}} simulates with the same seed. With \code{n = 30}, \code{delta = 5},
\code{pretest_effect = 2}, \code{rho = 0.6}, \code{sigma = 10}, \code{mean = 50},
\code{seed = 20260915}, \code{digits = 0}, and \code{limits = c(0, 100)}, they are the
bundled \link{solomon_example} data.

\strong{True values.} The \code{"truth"} attribute gives each estimand:
\itemize{
\item the treatment effect among unpretested participants, \code{delta};
\item among pretested participants, \code{delta + sens};
\item the Pretest x Treatment interaction, \code{sens};
\item the equal-weighted average treatment effect, \code{delta + sens / 2};
\item the pretest effect among controls, \code{pretest_effect};
\item among treated participants, \code{pretest_effect + sens};
\item and their average, the pretest main effect, \code{pretest_effect + sens / 2}.
}

Its rows follow the order of the \code{effects} table of \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}}.

Rounding and limits (\code{digits}, \code{limits}) make the scores look like test
scores, but they shift the true values slightly, and more so when many
scores reach a limit.

\strong{Choosing values.} Willson and Putnam (1982) found an average pretest
effect of about a fifth of a standard deviation in randomized studies;
the article "Planning a Solomon Study" discusses planning values.
}
\section{Designs with several treatments}{

A Solomon N-group design crosses k treatments and a control with
pretesting, giving 2(k + 1) groups: six for two treatments and eight for
three (Edmonds & Kennedy, 2017; Steyn, 2009). Give \code{delta} one effect per
treatment, named by treatment, for example \code{delta = c(A = 0.5, B = 0.2)}.
Without names the treatments are called \code{"T1"}, \code{"T2"}, and so on; the
control is \code{"Control"}. \code{sens} is one number for every treatment or one
per treatment, in the order of \code{delta}; a named \code{sens} is matched to the
treatments by name.

The model is the one above, with the effects \code{delta[j]} and \code{sens[j]} of
treatment j: each treatment differs from the control only by its own
effects. \code{n} is one size for every group, or 2(k + 1) sizes in this order:
the pretested treatments (in the order of \code{delta}), the pretested control,
the unpretested treatments, and the unpretested control. Sizes named by
group, as in the \code{"settings"} attribute (such as \code{"Pretested, A"}), or
named \code{n1}, \code{n2}, and so on for the groups in that order, may be given in
any order. Other names are refused. The groups are generated in the order
above, and the baselines are drawn before the errors.

The \code{treat} column is then a factor whose levels are the treatments and
\code{"Control"}, so the data go directly to
\code{fit_solomon_glm(y_post, treat, pretested, y_pre, control = "Control")}.
The \code{"truth"} attribute has one row for each treatment-control comparison
and contrast, with the columns \code{comparison} (such as \code{"A vs Control"}),
\code{contrast}, and \code{true_value}, in the order of the \code{effects} table of that
fit. Its last rows give the pretest effects: among controls
(\code{pretest_effect}), among the participants of each treatment j
(\code{pretest_effect + sens[j]}), and their average over the k + 1
conditions (\code{pretest_effect + sum(sens) / (k + 1)}, with comparison
\code{"All conditions"}). With one treatment effect the data and the \code{"truth"}
attribute are those described above.
}

